1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
Modern Marvels is going big and counting down.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:09,000
No series has celebrated technology over the years like we have.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
And now it's time to go gaga over gizmos

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
as we count down America's top 10 greatest gadgets.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
From the indispensable to the insane,

6
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
from golden oldies to new-fangled novelties,

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:26,000
our finalists and their creators

8
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
showcase the wonder of human ingenuity.

9
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:31,840
I reek with imagination. I just reek with it.

10
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:37,380
Be there for the big moment when we unveil the clever contraption that tops them all.

11
00:00:38,380 --> 00:00:44,300
Now, America's Greatest Gadgets on this edition of Modern Marvel's Mega Marvels Countdown.

12
00:00:44,301 --> 00:00:58,301
The world's multitude of gadgets epitomizes just how much we adore and depend on technology.

13
00:01:01,960 --> 00:01:08,160
Our stellar countdown features gizmos from time-tested icons to cutting-edge wizardry.

14
00:01:09,080 --> 00:01:11,900
But first things first, what exactly is a gadget?

15
00:01:11,901 --> 00:01:14,060
it.

16
00:01:14,061 --> 00:01:16,460
According to the Merriam-Webster dictionary,

17
00:01:16,461 --> 00:01:19,160
it's a small mechanical or electronic device

18
00:01:19,161 --> 00:01:24,740
with a practical use, but often thought of as a novelty.

19
00:01:24,741 --> 00:01:26,960
Our choice at number 10 does more than embrace

20
00:01:26,961 --> 00:01:28,960
that definition.

21
00:01:28,961 --> 00:01:32,960
It lampoons it in the most audacious fashion imaginable,

22
00:01:32,961 --> 00:01:38,000
the wild and wacky Rube Goldberg machine.

23
00:01:38,001 --> 00:01:41,120
Imagine a monstrosity designed to make a simple task

24
00:01:41,121 --> 00:01:43,580
needlessly complicated for comic effect.

25
00:01:45,860 --> 00:01:48,400
That's what the rock band OK Go crafted

26
00:01:48,401 --> 00:01:50,300
for a music video shot in LA,

27
00:01:50,301 --> 00:01:52,960
inspired by the popular invention cartoon series

28
00:01:52,961 --> 00:01:54,400
by artist Rube Goldberg.

29
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:59,300
If you want the smaller ones, you got the smaller.

30
00:01:59,301 --> 00:02:00,140
No, this is perfect.

31
00:02:00,141 --> 00:02:01,880
Yeah.

32
00:02:01,881 --> 00:02:04,000
When completed, this mega gadget

33
00:02:04,001 --> 00:02:06,700
will execute a bizarre chain reaction sequence

34
00:02:06,701 --> 00:02:09,240
in 50 separate steps.

35
00:02:09,241 --> 00:02:10,880
Does it always jump in the same area?

36
00:02:10,881 --> 00:02:14,120
It seems to be jumping on this area, not up here.

37
00:02:14,380 --> 00:02:17,640
A Rube Goldberg invention is something that's important because it's not important.

38
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:20,660
It's a device that takes something that is really simple,

39
00:02:21,020 --> 00:02:24,900
really easy to do in your everyday life, and make it really complex.

40
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:31,080
Case in point, this Goldberg machine, built especially for us to power up a TV.

41
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:35,840
The rat trap knocks the wheel forward, sending the CO2 rocket into the pendulum,

42
00:02:36,240 --> 00:02:39,580
which swings down and closes the pyramid, forcing Stonehenge to rise,

43
00:02:39,581 --> 00:02:41,680
which pushes the mallet to fall on the spatula,

44
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:44,300
which pulls a string that activates the gumball machine.

45
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:46,540
It kicks the tire that rolls to the cannon,

46
00:02:46,860 --> 00:02:49,040
shooting the shot put which funnels down the catcher rig,

47
00:02:49,360 --> 00:02:50,220
depressing the hand,

48
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:52,560
completing the task of turning on the TV.

49
00:02:57,060 --> 00:03:01,520
What if this quick release were on a pole here or something?

50
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:04,480
Each step of the Vans Goldberg machine

51
00:03:04,481 --> 00:03:07,520
must coincide with music cues throughout the four-minute song.

52
00:03:07,521 --> 00:03:11,680
No wonder this ridiculously complex gadget made our countdown.

53
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:15,420
The line that is sung in there is not going to happen

54
00:03:15,421 --> 00:03:17,400
until tink, tink, tink, tink is done no matter what.

55
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:21,220
The band's performance will be woven throughout the machine's operation,

56
00:03:21,620 --> 00:03:25,720
and all of it must be captured on camera in one continuous shot.

57
00:03:26,660 --> 00:03:30,340
Rube Goldberg himself would applaud a gadget this precise and ludicrous.

58
00:03:37,520 --> 00:03:42,700
The cartoonist who inspired our number 10 gadget earned an engineering degree in 1904,

59
00:03:43,380 --> 00:03:44,760
but answered a different calling.

60
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:48,520
He penned comics for the San Francisco Chronicle,

61
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:52,140
sending up the changing times of early 20th century life.

62
00:03:53,540 --> 00:03:58,040
In 1914, Rube debuted his satirical Invention series,

63
00:03:58,620 --> 00:04:00,580
greatly influenced by his engineering background.

64
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:06,380
He described his inventions as symbols of man's capacity

65
00:04:06,381 --> 00:04:10,280
for exerting maximum effort to accomplish minimum results.

66
00:04:12,560 --> 00:04:16,060
A typical invention consisted of several household items,

67
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:20,500
including animals, linked together to accomplish a mundane goal,

68
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:23,520
like cutting hair or using a napkin.

69
00:04:24,420 --> 00:04:28,580
His cartoons were particularly popular during the machine era,

70
00:04:28,581 --> 00:04:31,860
where lots of people were going out and trying to invent machines

71
00:04:31,861 --> 00:04:34,140
to solve any problem they possibly could.

72
00:04:34,141 --> 00:04:40,140
So Rube Goldberg was really making fun of the fact that, okay, we can design all this technology,

73
00:04:40,380 --> 00:04:44,140
but does it really make our lives simpler? That's left up to the reader.

74
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:48,360
Inventing a Rube Goldberg machine on paper is one thing.

75
00:04:48,880 --> 00:04:52,960
I'm hoping we can get past the first 15 seconds of the song.

76
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:55,100
Two, one, action!

77
00:04:55,660 --> 00:04:58,460
Making one of these gadgets work for real is another.

78
00:04:58,461 --> 00:05:11,200
It took 65 people three months to design and build our number 10 gadget.

79
00:05:13,020 --> 00:05:16,780
But take after take, it has refused to cooperate for the camera.

80
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:20,340
The tiniest imprecision brings the machine to a screeching halt.

81
00:05:24,560 --> 00:05:28,200
Filming one flawless four-minute continuous shot seems impossible.

82
00:05:28,460 --> 00:05:32,460
But as the production crew prepares for their 85th take,

83
00:05:32,461 --> 00:05:34,460
they believe they've worked out every bug

84
00:05:34,461 --> 00:05:35,460
in the bizarre machine.

85
00:05:35,461 --> 00:05:37,460
Roll camera.

86
00:05:37,461 --> 00:05:38,460
Finger.

87
00:05:38,461 --> 00:05:39,460
Finger.

88
00:05:39,461 --> 00:05:40,460
Oh, we got it.

89
00:05:40,461 --> 00:05:43,460
Three, two, one, action.

90
00:05:43,461 --> 00:05:47,460
A tap of a domino ignites the chain reaction,

91
00:05:47,461 --> 00:05:51,460
cueing the music along the way.

92
00:05:51,461 --> 00:05:56,460
Our number 10 gadget is really rolling, but for how long?

93
00:05:56,461 --> 00:05:57,360
But for how long?

94
00:06:04,560 --> 00:06:07,380
Each band member appears at four separate locations

95
00:06:07,381 --> 00:06:08,800
throughout the single take video.

96
00:06:17,780 --> 00:06:20,320
The sequence unfolds over two levels of the building.

97
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:40,460
And in the final seconds of the song, a barrage of effects rips across the studio.

98
00:06:46,440 --> 00:06:50,760
Then, the grand finale, a firing range of paint cannons.

99
00:06:56,460 --> 00:07:04,900
While this beast of a gadget rocks because there's genius in its complexity, the next

100
00:07:04,901 --> 00:07:11,080
entry in our countdown has endured for centuries because there's brilliance in its simplicity.

101
00:07:11,081 --> 00:07:18,640
Let's toast our gadget at number nine, a humble and handy corkscrew.

102
00:07:18,641 --> 00:07:23,140
We take this simple mechanical device for granted, but there's more to this icon among

103
00:07:23,141 --> 00:07:25,780
gadgets than meets the eye.

104
00:07:25,781 --> 00:07:30,840
have a fascinating history. They actually were inspired by guns.

105
00:07:30,841 --> 00:07:32,580
The guns in the Middle

106
00:07:32,581 --> 00:07:40,720
Ages came with a worm attached for extracting a bullet from the muzzle, and that was the

107
00:07:40,721 --> 00:07:44,018
inspiration behind the original corkscrews.

108
00:07:44,019 --> 00:07:47,240
These screwy gadgets aren't created equal.

109
00:07:48,000 --> 00:07:51,499
There are two types, the auger and helix.

110
00:07:51,500 --> 00:07:55,340
The helix consists of a metal spiral, usually

111
00:07:55,341 --> 00:07:57,490
comprised of at least five rings.

112
00:07:57,491 --> 00:08:01,660
When turned, the point of the helix worms its way into the cork,

113
00:08:01,661 --> 00:08:06,227
displacing as little of the cork material as possible.

114
00:08:06,228 --> 00:08:09,440
An auger is a screw with a solid shaft

115
00:08:09,441 --> 00:08:12,429
that when turned drills into the cork.

116
00:08:12,430 --> 00:08:16,520
This motion can tear at the cork tissue and possibly

117
00:08:16,521 --> 00:08:18,535
release debris into the wind.

118
00:08:18,536 --> 00:08:23,120
Well, good corkscrew, simple as it is, needs to be a well-designed

119
00:08:23,121 --> 00:08:26,960
gadget or frankly, you just shred the cork to smithereens.

120
00:08:29,420 --> 00:08:34,480
The Culinary Institute of America in St. Helena, California, is home to one of the

121
00:08:34,481 --> 00:08:38,940
most famous corkscrew collections in the world with over 1,800 devices.

122
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:47,680
The structure was once the Christian Brothers Winery, where beginning in 1949, its legendary

123
00:08:47,681 --> 00:08:53,260
Cellar Master, Brother Timothy, gathered corkscrews dating back as far as the 18th century.

124
00:08:54,260 --> 00:08:57,520
Brother Timothy was really interested in the evolution of this gadget.

125
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:01,520
He wanted to know the most efficient way of opening a bottle of wine.

126
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:05,460
A lot of these corkscrews come in a whole variety of shapes and sizes,

127
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:07,740
and many of these are novelty corkscrews.

128
00:09:07,741 --> 00:09:12,420
As you can see, we have a monk looking very pious and sanctimonious here,

129
00:09:12,500 --> 00:09:15,840
but you remove his head, and he's got a corkscrew under there.

130
00:09:15,841 --> 00:09:18,840
These are examples of pocket corkscrews.

131
00:09:18,841 --> 00:09:21,840
What we have here are folding bows.

132
00:09:21,841 --> 00:09:23,840
They are single pull corkscrews,

133
00:09:23,841 --> 00:09:26,840
as well as double finger pulling corkscrews.

134
00:09:26,841 --> 00:09:29,840
We also have a little left-handed corkscrew

135
00:09:29,841 --> 00:09:31,840
that the helix goes in the opposite direction

136
00:09:31,841 --> 00:09:34,840
than it would for a right-handed person.

137
00:09:34,841 --> 00:09:36,840
In addition to wine,

138
00:09:36,841 --> 00:09:38,840
corkscrews have opened everything from medicine

139
00:09:38,841 --> 00:09:40,840
to cosmetic bottles.

140
00:09:40,841 --> 00:09:42,840
You'd hardly imagine that there would be a bottle of wine

141
00:09:42,841 --> 00:09:44,840
for this corkscrew, but this is actually used

142
00:09:44,841 --> 00:09:47,840
This is actually used to remove corks from perfume bottles.

143
00:09:50,240 --> 00:09:53,740
But this iconic gadget's days may be numbered,

144
00:09:53,741 --> 00:09:56,940
thanks to new bottle openers, like the air-pressured cork lifter.

145
00:09:56,941 --> 00:10:01,440
You want to be very careful here, because there's a lot of air pressure going in with each pump.

146
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:07,140
Cork screws have a precarious future.

147
00:10:07,141 --> 00:10:12,040
More and more wines are bottled under a screw cap that don't require a corkscrew.

148
00:10:12,041 --> 00:10:16,620
Here's a gadget that may, in a hundred years from now, disappear.

149
00:10:17,100 --> 00:10:19,380
And that would be sad indeed.

150
00:10:19,860 --> 00:10:21,120
We second that emotion.

151
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:28,840
We're celebrating the innovative world of gizmos, doohickeys, and thingamajigs.

152
00:10:29,720 --> 00:10:33,480
Counting down the top ten greatest gadgets our series has ever featured.

153
00:10:34,740 --> 00:10:39,700
And we won't be through tinkering until we unveil our greatest gadget of all at number one.

154
00:10:42,040 --> 00:10:45,240
The zany Rube Goldberg machine set our countdown in motion

155
00:10:45,241 --> 00:10:49,260
at number 10, followed by the handy-dandy corkscrew

156
00:10:49,261 --> 00:10:51,560
that pulled out all the stops at number 9.

157
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:56,440
Our number 8 gadget is an electronic wonder

158
00:10:56,441 --> 00:11:00,640
that can virtually see through walls, the Tealink sensor.

159
00:11:02,540 --> 00:11:05,300
Imagine a futuristic gadget that promises

160
00:11:05,301 --> 00:11:08,760
to revolutionize battle strategies and law enforcement tactics,

161
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:12,020
enabling soldiers and the police to detect

162
00:11:12,021 --> 00:11:14,020
concealed bad guys lurking nearby.

163
00:11:17,300 --> 00:11:21,020
This sensor, invented by the Tealinks Corporation,

164
00:11:21,021 --> 00:11:23,340
is answering that challenge.

165
00:11:23,341 --> 00:11:27,300
It's a wonder gadget that uses a highly refined form of radar,

166
00:11:27,301 --> 00:11:30,460
a lot like Superman uses his X-ray vision.

167
00:11:30,461 --> 00:11:34,420
This device is specifically designed for detection

168
00:11:34,421 --> 00:11:36,300
of the objects behind a wall.

169
00:11:36,301 --> 00:11:40,060
This is a very sensitive device for any slightest movement.

170
00:11:40,061 --> 00:11:42,520
We not only use that for the detection of the people

171
00:11:42,521 --> 00:11:45,800
behind a wall, you can detect even the breathing of a person

172
00:11:45,801 --> 00:11:49,160
who has been hidden behind a wall.

173
00:11:49,161 --> 00:11:51,100
The Tealink sensor can work its magic

174
00:11:51,101 --> 00:11:55,740
through walls made of wood, concrete, even steel.

175
00:11:55,741 --> 00:11:58,240
It can be applied directly to a wall,

176
00:11:58,241 --> 00:12:02,580
worn by police or military personnel,

177
00:12:02,581 --> 00:12:05,660
or moved into position by this intrepid robot,

178
00:12:05,661 --> 00:12:07,960
when the danger behind a wall is so great

179
00:12:07,961 --> 00:12:09,960
that no human dare approach.

180
00:12:16,960 --> 00:12:18,960
When the sensor is placed on a wall,

181
00:12:18,961 --> 00:12:21,960
it transmits very low-power signals.

182
00:12:21,961 --> 00:12:24,960
And this low-power signal travels in the media,

183
00:12:24,961 --> 00:12:26,960
whether it's wall or air.

184
00:12:26,961 --> 00:12:28,960
When it hits the next object,

185
00:12:28,961 --> 00:12:30,960
which is a person or an object,

186
00:12:30,961 --> 00:12:32,960
then it reflects some of that back.

187
00:12:33,960 --> 00:12:35,960
The data the sensors gather are conveyed

188
00:12:35,961 --> 00:12:40,340
to standard computer monitors or miniature head-mounted devices.

189
00:12:42,600 --> 00:12:46,600
This impressive gadget produces just one-tenth the power of a cell phone,

190
00:12:47,200 --> 00:12:49,440
but the tech it harnesses is so effective

191
00:12:49,441 --> 00:12:53,400
that virtually anyone lurking on the other side of a wall can be located.

192
00:12:55,920 --> 00:12:56,620
Go.

193
00:12:57,680 --> 00:12:59,540
All right, I'm in the house now.

194
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:04,280
In this experiment, a test subject enters the building

195
00:13:04,281 --> 00:13:07,400
as T-Link sensors emit their signals on the exterior wall.

196
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:12,500
And I'm about 30 feet from the radar.

197
00:13:13,100 --> 00:13:15,440
A technician stands ready to analyze the data.

198
00:13:16,060 --> 00:13:18,380
And I'm going to the first room.

199
00:13:19,200 --> 00:13:20,760
The sensors detect any motion

200
00:13:20,761 --> 00:13:23,320
and can calculate the distance between the wall

201
00:13:23,321 --> 00:13:24,580
and the subject that's moving.

202
00:13:25,420 --> 00:13:26,840
Still moving backwards.

203
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:31,080
I'm coming towards the end of the house.

204
00:13:31,081 --> 00:13:34,260
and now about 60 feet.

205
00:13:35,760 --> 00:13:39,760
Going through three interior walls and one exterior wall,

206
00:13:40,180 --> 00:13:41,180
and I'll go back.

207
00:13:42,360 --> 00:13:45,480
A graphic display of a person's movements can also be computed.

208
00:13:53,520 --> 00:13:57,160
It's easy to see how a gadget with such jaw-dropping technology

209
00:13:57,161 --> 00:13:58,620
won its place on our countdown.

210
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:09,200
Its makers believe its ability to monitor unseen threats through walls will save innumerable

211
00:14:09,201 --> 00:14:10,200
lives.

212
00:14:10,201 --> 00:14:15,680
It will save lives on the Army.

213
00:14:15,681 --> 00:14:18,680
It definitely needs these kind of technologies.

214
00:14:18,681 --> 00:14:26,120
While the T-Link sensor poses a threat to hidden bad guys, the gadget in our number

215
00:14:26,121 --> 00:14:30,569
seven spot was once considered a threat to national security.

216
00:14:30,570 --> 00:14:32,320
Say hello to that fondly

217
00:14:32,321 --> 00:14:34,784
remembered fad of the 90s, Furby.

218
00:14:34,785 --> 00:14:39,040
This critter made our cut because it foreshadowed today's

219
00:14:39,041 --> 00:14:44,600
reliance on miniaturized electronics, epitomizing the American genius for packing a wealth of

220
00:14:44,601 --> 00:14:48,514
high-tech into the smallest of packages.

221
00:14:48,515 --> 00:14:52,820
No discussion of the 1990s would be complete

222
00:14:52,821 --> 00:14:57,260
without talking about Furby, introduced in 1998.

223
00:14:57,261 --> 00:15:01,560
It was the world's first autonomous robotic pet.

224
00:15:01,561 --> 00:15:05,760
So as a robot, it's able to move things

225
00:15:05,761 --> 00:15:08,200
like its ears and its mouth and its eyes.

226
00:15:08,201 --> 00:15:11,840
It also speaks, and it speaks a language called Furbish.

227
00:15:15,540 --> 00:15:18,040
The more you played with your furry Furby friend,

228
00:15:18,041 --> 00:15:21,280
it would speak less Furbish and seem to learn English.

229
00:15:22,820 --> 00:15:24,620
One, two, one!

230
00:15:25,960 --> 00:15:31,000
What it really did was gradually reveal up to 100 pre-programmed English words.

231
00:15:31,700 --> 00:15:38,560
Because of this, there was a common misconception that Furbies repeated the words set around them.

232
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:43,340
And some government officials thought the gadget could threaten national security.

233
00:15:44,920 --> 00:15:51,980
In fact, the National Security Agency in Maryland banned its employees from bringing our number seven entry

234
00:15:51,981 --> 00:15:55,020
into its buildings in January 1999.

235
00:15:57,460 --> 00:16:00,100
People didn't really know what was going on here.

236
00:16:00,160 --> 00:16:02,480
They didn't know how much power was inside this thing,

237
00:16:02,540 --> 00:16:03,700
so it scared some people.

238
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:04,960
Be scared.

239
00:16:05,620 --> 00:16:07,820
Despite Furby's treasonous reputation,

240
00:16:08,580 --> 00:16:11,340
more than $15 million were sold by the end of the 90s.

241
00:16:15,100 --> 00:16:18,800
The Furby cost about $35 in 1998,

242
00:16:18,801 --> 00:16:21,380
which is pretty expensive for a toy,

243
00:16:21,381 --> 00:16:26,720
But when you take apart a Furby and look inside, you realize why it costs so much.

244
00:16:26,760 --> 00:16:30,020
It really was a miracle of modern technology.

245
00:16:31,080 --> 00:16:34,720
The Furby came fully equipped with touch sensors on the front and back,

246
00:16:35,540 --> 00:16:39,400
a microphone to pick up sound, a sensor to detect light,

247
00:16:40,420 --> 00:16:42,900
an infrared port to communicate with other Furbys,

248
00:16:43,060 --> 00:16:46,700
and even a sensor that could detect upside-down movements.

249
00:16:46,701 --> 00:16:54,740
One of the things that let them keep the cost down was a design feature that used just one

250
00:16:54,741 --> 00:16:58,346
electric motor that drove a cam system.

251
00:16:58,347 --> 00:17:03,340
So this single motor with the cams can drive the ears,

252
00:17:03,980 --> 00:17:10,420
the eyelids, the mouth, and the ability of the Furby to tip up and kind of move forward.

253
00:17:10,421 --> 00:17:13,420
The microprocessor has about four times the power

254
00:17:13,520 --> 00:17:15,920
of the Apollo moon lander.

255
00:17:16,020 --> 00:17:18,660
It's an amazing amount of processing power

256
00:17:18,760 --> 00:17:21,960
to fit into such a small and inexpensive package.

257
00:17:22,060 --> 00:17:24,860
Herbie isn't the only gadget on our countdown

258
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:28,480
that was once all the rage and is now just a nostalgic memory.

259
00:17:33,580 --> 00:17:35,420
Before we resume our countdown,

260
00:17:35,520 --> 00:17:37,380
let's salute one mesmerizing gadget

261
00:17:37,480 --> 00:17:39,580
that's familiar to everyone, but just

262
00:17:39,581 --> 00:17:42,680
but just missed our cut.

263
00:17:42,681 --> 00:17:46,300
That psychedelic icon that captures the spirit of the 1960s

264
00:17:46,301 --> 00:17:50,480
in a bottle, the Lava Lamp.

265
00:17:50,481 --> 00:17:53,020
Part of its intrigue is that the company that makes it

266
00:17:53,021 --> 00:17:58,280
won't divulge precisely what's inside that makes it work.

267
00:17:58,281 --> 00:18:00,020
Employees at the Lava Lamp factory

268
00:18:00,021 --> 00:18:02,260
take an oath of secrecy to ensure

269
00:18:02,261 --> 00:18:07,060
that the mysterious ingredients stay a mystery.

270
00:18:07,061 --> 00:18:09,160
We don't really know what's inside here

271
00:18:09,161 --> 00:18:11,680
because it's a closely guarded trade secret.

272
00:18:11,681 --> 00:18:14,600
But what we do know is that the two substances

273
00:18:14,601 --> 00:18:16,420
are very close in density,

274
00:18:16,421 --> 00:18:19,340
and one of them is a waxy substance

275
00:18:19,341 --> 00:18:21,840
and the other is a liquid.

276
00:18:21,841 --> 00:18:24,520
If we pop the top, we can actually separate them

277
00:18:24,521 --> 00:18:26,340
and see the differences.

278
00:18:31,780 --> 00:18:33,440
Here comes the wax.

279
00:18:35,180 --> 00:18:38,240
The wax is already starting to harden,

280
00:18:38,241 --> 00:18:40,940
And you can see it's just like candle wax that's been melted.

281
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:46,800
The key to the motion of the blobs within the lamp is the light bulb in the base.

282
00:18:47,660 --> 00:18:50,160
It keeps the wax from hardening and sinking to the bottom.

283
00:18:50,820 --> 00:18:59,780
The heat melts the wax and then expands it just a little bit so that it's less dense than the liquid around it and it can rise.

284
00:18:59,920 --> 00:19:06,920
As it gets up to the top, it cools, contracts just a little bit, gets more dense and is able to fall back down to the bottom.

285
00:19:06,921 --> 00:19:11,360
And that heating and cooling, expanding, contracting process

286
00:19:11,361 --> 00:19:14,620
is what gives this its amazing, mesmerizing look.

287
00:19:16,080 --> 00:19:19,220
While the lava lamp is a gadget designed to please the eye,

288
00:19:19,900 --> 00:19:23,800
we resume our countdown with a gadget that's a melodic trait for the ear.

289
00:19:24,580 --> 00:19:28,920
Chiming in at number six, it's Benjamin Franklin's forgotten musical wonder,

290
00:19:29,520 --> 00:19:30,720
the glass harmonica.

291
00:19:32,100 --> 00:19:36,800
The idea for Ben's brainchild hit him at a concert in England in 1757.

292
00:19:36,920 --> 00:19:40,780
where a musician played tunes on the rims of wine glasses.

293
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:45,520
Franklin loved the sound of the vibrating glass

294
00:19:45,521 --> 00:19:48,280
but wondered if there was a better way to produce it.

295
00:19:50,160 --> 00:19:53,760
So he thought, first of all, the water tuning's got to go way too much trouble.

296
00:19:54,120 --> 00:19:56,580
So if you make the glasses the correct size in the first place,

297
00:19:56,720 --> 00:19:59,340
then you can dispense with that and you'll end up with a set

298
00:19:59,341 --> 00:20:02,420
ranging from a relatively large glass for your base end

299
00:20:02,421 --> 00:20:04,080
to small glasses for the high end.

300
00:20:04,081 --> 00:20:08,920
This would allow a musician to play complex chords in addition to single note melodies.

301
00:20:10,280 --> 00:20:19,400
Instead of the musician rotating his fingers, 37 glasses of varying diameter and thickness would rotate, turned by a foot-powered spindle.

302
00:20:21,360 --> 00:20:24,700
Franklin unveiled his glass harmonica in 1761.

303
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:38,000
His gadget's ethereal sound created a sensation in Europe.

304
00:20:42,120 --> 00:20:44,300
Marie Antoinette played the harmonica.

305
00:20:45,580 --> 00:20:51,160
The renowned German physician Franz Mesmer used its heavenly tones to mesmerize his patients.

306
00:20:53,840 --> 00:20:57,480
Our number six gadget also mesmerized a renowned composer.

307
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:02,740
Mozart's first exposure was actually at Mesmer's house.

308
00:21:02,741 --> 00:21:05,740
the Mesmers and the Mozarts were friends.

309
00:21:07,140 --> 00:21:09,740
Mozart wrote two pieces for the glass harmonica,

310
00:21:10,260 --> 00:21:11,100
including this one.

311
00:21:19,740 --> 00:21:22,680
Beethoven and Haydn also composed for Franklin's gadget.

312
00:21:25,360 --> 00:21:27,760
Franklin himself excelled at it.

313
00:21:27,880 --> 00:21:29,960
He took one with him often when he traveled.

314
00:21:29,961 --> 00:21:32,180
He brought it back to Philadelphia.

315
00:21:32,740 --> 00:21:35,900
There's a story that his wife Deborah woke up one night

316
00:21:35,901 --> 00:21:37,840
and she heard the music of the harmonica.

317
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:41,300
It was Ben practicing and she assumed she had died and gone to heaven.

318
00:21:45,800 --> 00:21:50,020
Despite the wild enthusiasm, the instrument's life was surprisingly brief.

319
00:21:51,860 --> 00:21:54,000
They eventually sort of lost out to the piano,

320
00:21:54,300 --> 00:21:57,720
which had come on strong by the late 18th, early 19th century.

321
00:21:57,721 --> 00:22:02,200
A much louder, much more adaptable instrument to the larger and larger orchestras

322
00:22:02,201 --> 00:22:03,140
that were being used.

323
00:22:03,520 --> 00:22:04,640
The harmonica was always very much

324
00:22:04,641 --> 00:22:07,620
kind of a very quiet chamber-like instrument.

325
00:22:08,480 --> 00:22:11,060
There were also rumors that the gadget's tones,

326
00:22:11,340 --> 00:22:13,300
or perhaps the lead used in the glass,

327
00:22:14,100 --> 00:22:14,920
caused insanity.

328
00:22:16,040 --> 00:22:17,940
Personally, I think they have that backwards.

329
00:22:18,240 --> 00:22:19,740
I think you start a little crazy

330
00:22:19,741 --> 00:22:21,080
and then you want to play this.

331
00:22:23,480 --> 00:22:25,560
Harmonica virtuoso William Zeitler

332
00:22:25,561 --> 00:22:26,680
has made it his mission

333
00:22:26,681 --> 00:22:28,580
to resurrect our number six gadget.

334
00:22:29,940 --> 00:22:31,720
He performs around the world,

335
00:22:32,200 --> 00:22:34,340
and composes original pieces for it.

336
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:38,620
I'd really like to get this instrument off of the endangered musical instrument list.

337
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:47,100
There aren't many places to find precision-tuned glass bowls these days,

338
00:22:47,960 --> 00:22:51,060
so Zeitler turned to CalGlass in Costa Mesa, California,

339
00:22:51,620 --> 00:22:54,440
which normally makes glass scientific instruments.

340
00:22:56,940 --> 00:22:58,780
After being shaped on a lathe,

341
00:22:58,781 --> 00:23:02,440
Each glass bowl is sanded until it reaches the perfect pitch.

342
00:23:05,360 --> 00:23:06,760
Pretty close, too.

343
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:13,440
A mesmerizing sound, an intriguing backstory, and a legendary creator.

344
00:23:14,060 --> 00:23:19,160
A harmonious combination that earns the glass harmonica its place of honor on our countdown.

345
00:23:28,780 --> 00:23:37,140
In a world teeming with mechanical and electronic wizardry, it's not easy selecting our top

346
00:23:37,141 --> 00:23:42,740
ten picks for the greatest gadgets we've ever presented.

347
00:23:42,741 --> 00:23:46,320
Hopefully our choices so far come close to your own.

348
00:23:46,321 --> 00:23:52,600
From the Rube Goldberg machine at number ten, to Ben Franklin's Armonica at number six.

349
00:23:52,601 --> 00:23:56,960
And maybe you remember this rude awakening of a gizmo that didn't make our cut, but

350
00:23:56,961 --> 00:23:59,720
merits a mention before we press forward.

351
00:23:59,721 --> 00:24:01,520
The flying alarm clock.

352
00:24:01,521 --> 00:24:05,300
You set it for a pre-wake time, and what happens

353
00:24:05,301 --> 00:24:09,100
is a horrible blaring noise emits from it.

354
00:24:09,101 --> 00:24:14,260
And this rotor lifts off the base like a helicopter,

355
00:24:14,261 --> 00:24:16,340
flies around the room, and the noise

356
00:24:16,341 --> 00:24:20,300
will keep blaring at you until you find this piece

357
00:24:20,301 --> 00:24:23,000
and put it back onto the base.

358
00:24:23,001 --> 00:24:25,560
The flying alarm clock has become a popular choice

359
00:24:25,561 --> 00:24:27,600
among gizmo-loving early risers.

360
00:24:30,640 --> 00:24:32,400
But not every gadget on our countdown

361
00:24:32,401 --> 00:24:35,380
earned its place because it's a best seller.

362
00:24:35,381 --> 00:24:37,920
The contraptions coming in at number five

363
00:24:37,921 --> 00:24:40,980
never got any further than the inventor's workshop.

364
00:24:40,981 --> 00:24:43,120
The wacky, wonderful, unsold gadgets

365
00:24:43,121 --> 00:24:45,700
of our favorite tinkerer, Brent Farley.

366
00:24:48,680 --> 00:24:51,040
Brent's gadgets are created in his cluttered basement

367
00:24:51,041 --> 00:24:52,740
in Baltimore.

368
00:24:52,741 --> 00:24:54,680
And even though they haven't sold,

369
00:24:54,681 --> 00:24:59,760
They made our countdown because they say so much about the energy and optimism of the inventive mind.

370
00:25:00,640 --> 00:25:05,940
Over the years, I've probably came up with about 200 gadgets that I consider to be valid.

371
00:25:07,060 --> 00:25:12,920
This veteran stagehand's uphill battle to sell his unique creations hasn't dampened his inventive urge.

372
00:25:13,940 --> 00:25:15,780
The ideas just keep coming.

373
00:25:17,840 --> 00:25:20,000
Somewhere on the shelf, there's room for a new gadget.

374
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:21,960
And this is where I'm coming into the picture.

375
00:25:21,961 --> 00:25:25,158
I don't want to sound like I'm a cock inventor, but I reek with imagination.

376
00:25:25,159 --> 00:25:26,000
I just reek with it.

377
00:25:26,900 --> 00:25:29,420
Wherever Brent sees a need, he creates a gadget.

378
00:25:30,420 --> 00:25:34,800
I've had trouble with my windshield wipers through time, not actually working as they should have.

379
00:25:34,860 --> 00:25:37,340
They either go this way or they flip-flop and didn't clean the windshield.

380
00:25:37,740 --> 00:25:39,540
I came up with the Zigzag Windshield Wiper.

381
00:25:39,680 --> 00:25:44,220
This is designed never to flip-flop and or malfunction.

382
00:25:44,820 --> 00:25:46,840
This gadget here I call the Wing Walker.

383
00:25:46,841 --> 00:25:49,680
It's meant to give you the most radical ride you'll ever,

384
00:25:49,681 --> 00:25:52,840
ever get riding on a skateboard.

385
00:25:52,841 --> 00:25:54,520
This here, I call it the Arning Mitt.

386
00:25:54,521 --> 00:25:57,320
Simply put your hand in, pick it up, there you go.

387
00:25:57,321 --> 00:25:58,860
You're rubbing your clothes.

388
00:25:58,861 --> 00:26:01,860
You can get around collars, get in small places.

389
00:26:01,861 --> 00:26:03,420
Call it the Arning Mitt.

390
00:26:03,421 --> 00:26:06,320
Here's my trash can design.

391
00:26:06,321 --> 00:26:08,700
It's meant to carry the most amount of trash

392
00:26:08,701 --> 00:26:11,240
that you could possibly carry in the most efficient way.

393
00:26:11,241 --> 00:26:14,500
This is a mini prototype, easy steering, easy mobility.

394
00:26:14,501 --> 00:26:16,840
This really utilizes your space.

395
00:26:16,841 --> 00:26:20,940
A very, very small person can carry a lot, a lot of weight

396
00:26:20,941 --> 00:26:24,280
with my trash cart.

397
00:26:24,281 --> 00:26:27,480
Some of Brent's gadgets find use in his home.

398
00:26:27,481 --> 00:26:29,000
This is my shower gadget.

399
00:26:29,001 --> 00:26:29,840
Here's the mirror.

400
00:26:29,841 --> 00:26:32,560
The mirror goes up and down to your desired height.

401
00:26:32,561 --> 00:26:35,280
I've got the lights on the right and left side of the face

402
00:26:35,281 --> 00:26:38,540
to get a really good close shave.

403
00:26:38,541 --> 00:26:41,020
I have my razor that comes in and out of my housing here

404
00:26:41,021 --> 00:26:41,820
that goes up and down.

405
00:26:41,821 --> 00:26:43,120
It's out of the way.

406
00:26:43,121 --> 00:26:46,040
My wash rag comes up and down and goes out of the way.

407
00:26:46,041 --> 00:26:49,140
Here's my toothpaste, and here is my toothbrush.

408
00:26:49,600 --> 00:26:53,500
All within a unit, and I'd hate to take a shower without it.

409
00:26:54,340 --> 00:27:01,720
When I start to get an idea, it's because I have seen something that I know I can do a little better.

410
00:27:03,900 --> 00:27:08,040
While Brent dreams of inventing a gadget that will someday make it onto store shelves,

411
00:27:09,120 --> 00:27:14,580
the gadget in our number four spot has scored more than 100 million sales over the past half century.

412
00:27:16,040 --> 00:27:19,980
It's the timeless two-knobbed teledoodler, the Etch-A-Sketch.

413
00:27:23,260 --> 00:27:25,800
Its origins date back to the 1950s,

414
00:27:26,020 --> 00:27:30,080
when inventor Andre Casanez devised a gizmo in his Paris garage.

415
00:27:30,680 --> 00:27:34,500
He called l'écran magique, the magic screen.

416
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:38,400
Etch-A-Sketch, that's all you'll never tire of.

417
00:27:39,680 --> 00:27:43,240
In 1960, the Ohio Art Company brought it to America,

418
00:27:43,241 --> 00:27:47,240
renamed it, and made it a gotta-have gadget.

419
00:27:47,241 --> 00:27:49,240
It was very, very popular in the 60s

420
00:27:49,241 --> 00:27:52,240
because the whole nation was TV crazy.

421
00:27:52,241 --> 00:27:54,240
Everyone was crazy about the television.

422
00:27:54,241 --> 00:27:57,240
And here was a toy that, you know, was on the market

423
00:27:57,241 --> 00:28:00,240
that basically looked like a TV, kids could draw on it.

424
00:28:00,241 --> 00:28:03,240
Also, Etch-a-Sketch was one of the first toys

425
00:28:03,241 --> 00:28:06,240
to actually have a commercial on television.

426
00:28:08,240 --> 00:28:10,240
One reason this icon made our countdown

427
00:28:10,241 --> 00:28:12,240
is that it's remained relatively unchanged.

428
00:28:12,241 --> 00:28:15,080
changed since its debut.

429
00:28:15,081 --> 00:28:17,180
If you've ever wondered what's inside,

430
00:28:17,181 --> 00:28:20,480
we have the taken apart Etch-A-Sketch here.

431
00:28:20,481 --> 00:28:21,560
And it's really simple.

432
00:28:21,561 --> 00:28:25,580
You've got a stylus, which is what pushes the powder

433
00:28:25,581 --> 00:28:28,300
off the glass to make it draw.

434
00:28:28,301 --> 00:28:30,680
We have a pair of rails.

435
00:28:30,681 --> 00:28:32,600
And really what we have is the essence

436
00:28:32,601 --> 00:28:35,160
of a computer plotter here.

437
00:28:35,161 --> 00:28:37,360
And then we have the magic ingredient

438
00:28:37,361 --> 00:28:39,180
that allows you to erase.

439
00:28:39,181 --> 00:28:42,820
a combination of aluminum beads and aluminum powder.

440
00:28:42,821 --> 00:28:43,940
And you might be wondering,

441
00:28:43,941 --> 00:28:46,620
why would this aluminum powder stick to the glass?

442
00:28:46,621 --> 00:28:49,380
And it's because this aluminum powder sticks to everything.

443
00:28:49,381 --> 00:28:51,980
It's really clingy stuff.

444
00:28:53,360 --> 00:28:55,020
The left and right knobs enable you

445
00:28:55,021 --> 00:28:57,300
to draw horizontal and vertical lines.

446
00:28:58,660 --> 00:29:01,680
Most of us have mastered stairs, maybe a house.

447
00:29:02,800 --> 00:29:04,860
But Etch-a-Sketch artists like Kristoff

448
00:29:04,861 --> 00:29:06,960
can transform our number four gadget

449
00:29:06,961 --> 00:29:11,160
into much more than an addictive toy.

450
00:29:11,161 --> 00:29:14,580
The artistry you see here requires one basic but important

451
00:29:14,581 --> 00:29:17,540
technique.

452
00:29:17,541 --> 00:29:21,120
Basically, the secret is you have to use both knobs

453
00:29:21,121 --> 00:29:22,400
at the same time.

454
00:29:22,401 --> 00:29:25,000
If you use the knobs inward like this,

455
00:29:25,001 --> 00:29:26,580
you create a diagonal line.

456
00:29:26,581 --> 00:29:30,480
Now, if I were to use the knobs side to side in motion,

457
00:29:30,481 --> 00:29:32,380
you're creating the other diagonal line.

458
00:29:32,381 --> 00:29:36,940
And then, if you use the force, you can create a circle.

459
00:29:36,941 --> 00:29:43,100
The Etch-a-Sketch is one marvel of a gadget that refuses to fade away,

460
00:29:43,820 --> 00:29:46,540
making it a worthy pick for our number four spot.

461
00:29:52,420 --> 00:29:54,600
We're saluting our favorite contraptions,

462
00:29:55,420 --> 00:29:58,180
counting down the top ten most innovative, iconic,

463
00:29:58,620 --> 00:30:01,240
or mind-blowing gizmos our series has ever featured.

464
00:30:03,060 --> 00:30:05,160
And the parade of wizardry won't end

465
00:30:05,161 --> 00:30:08,400
until we reveal our greatest gadget of all at number one.

466
00:30:11,480 --> 00:30:15,480
Brent Farley's unsold inventions kept us hopping at number five,

467
00:30:16,300 --> 00:30:18,920
and the Etch-a-Sketch drew the line at number four.

468
00:30:19,920 --> 00:30:22,680
Now let's fire up our choice at number three,

469
00:30:23,300 --> 00:30:25,860
the stylish and reliable Zippo lighter.

470
00:30:28,520 --> 00:30:31,280
It's the Zippo lighter's famous windproof flame

471
00:30:31,281 --> 00:30:32,980
that made it such a successful gadget,

472
00:30:32,981 --> 00:30:35,140
and an American icon.

473
00:30:36,280 --> 00:30:38,200
It's produced at Zippo's headquarters

474
00:30:38,201 --> 00:30:39,520
in Bradford, Pennsylvania.

475
00:30:41,120 --> 00:30:43,280
Virtually all of our pocket lighters

476
00:30:43,281 --> 00:30:44,820
are manufactured in America.

477
00:30:45,460 --> 00:30:49,580
Since 1932, we've made over 450 million lighters

478
00:30:49,581 --> 00:30:50,900
in this factory.

479
00:30:51,980 --> 00:30:53,320
Inside a Zippo case,

480
00:30:53,600 --> 00:30:55,240
a wick runs from the top of the chimney

481
00:30:55,241 --> 00:30:56,240
into the fuel tank.

482
00:30:57,600 --> 00:30:58,880
The wick acts as a pump,

483
00:30:59,500 --> 00:31:00,880
pulling a predetermined amount

484
00:31:00,881 --> 00:31:03,080
of specially blended fuel up from the tank.

485
00:31:04,960 --> 00:31:08,480
To light a Zippo, the striker wheel rotates against the flint.

486
00:31:09,860 --> 00:31:12,900
Its ridged edge chips off microscopic metal fragments,

487
00:31:13,140 --> 00:31:13,960
creating sparks.

488
00:31:15,300 --> 00:31:18,500
These sparks light the fuel along the wick, producing a flame.

489
00:31:20,580 --> 00:31:23,180
The lighter's chimney protects the wick from wind,

490
00:31:23,660 --> 00:31:27,380
but also allows just enough air in through its strategically-sized holes

491
00:31:27,381 --> 00:31:30,980
so the flame stays lit, even in windy conditions.

492
00:31:33,720 --> 00:31:37,320
Workers fit each striking mechanism into its case by hand,

493
00:31:37,620 --> 00:31:40,260
one at a time, confirming that each lighter

494
00:31:40,261 --> 00:31:41,660
opens and closes properly.

495
00:31:42,560 --> 00:31:45,400
Then each lighter must pass the Zippo click test.

496
00:31:47,440 --> 00:31:49,980
We check the signature click, and there it is.

497
00:31:50,980 --> 00:31:52,660
If you don't hear it, it don't go.

498
00:31:54,180 --> 00:31:55,520
See, this one's kind of dragging.

499
00:31:55,620 --> 00:31:56,620
It's not doing its thing.

500
00:31:57,380 --> 00:31:59,380
Go in there and you spread that.

501
00:32:01,380 --> 00:32:02,380
And then you get the click.

502
00:32:02,381 --> 00:32:05,380
The click that the lighter makes when the top is opened,

503
00:32:05,381 --> 00:32:08,380
that's probably one of the most recognizable sounds

504
00:32:08,381 --> 00:32:12,380
that you can have in your head or even think about.

505
00:32:12,381 --> 00:32:16,380
Even when it's dark, you can hear the Zippo click,

506
00:32:16,381 --> 00:32:18,380
and you immediately know that's a Zippo.

507
00:32:20,380 --> 00:32:22,380
Now this is more like it.

508
00:32:22,381 --> 00:32:24,380
That man has a Zippo.

509
00:32:24,381 --> 00:32:27,540
Our number three gadget first became a pop culture icon

510
00:32:27,541 --> 00:32:30,340
during World War II, when a Zippo lighter

511
00:32:30,341 --> 00:32:33,740
was in every GI's pocket, courtesy of the U.S. government.

512
00:32:34,860 --> 00:32:38,120
Its trusty windproof flame could light a cigarette anywhere,

513
00:32:38,820 --> 00:32:41,500
and soldiers found countless other uses for it

514
00:32:41,501 --> 00:32:45,200
on the battlefield, from lighting signal fires

515
00:32:45,201 --> 00:32:47,740
to tapping out secret codes.

516
00:32:49,120 --> 00:32:51,100
Tried and tested by soldiers on the war front,

517
00:32:51,780 --> 00:32:53,380
the Zippo became a legend at home,

518
00:32:53,381 --> 00:32:57,680
and now occupies a revered place in America's popular landscape.

519
00:32:57,681 --> 00:32:59,080
Works every time.

520
00:32:59,081 --> 00:33:04,080
The gadget occupying the number two spot in our countdown

521
00:33:04,081 --> 00:33:06,980
is so much a part of our lives that we forget it's there.

522
00:33:06,981 --> 00:33:12,280
That low-tech, indispensable wonder, the zipper.

523
00:33:12,281 --> 00:33:18,780
No other gizmo that made our cut is as handy or underappreciated.

524
00:33:18,781 --> 00:33:22,880
Prior to the zipper, fabric closure devices ranged

525
00:33:22,881 --> 00:33:25,640
from the button to lacing.

526
00:33:25,641 --> 00:33:30,760
Then in 1851, Elias Howe, inventor of the sewing machine,

527
00:33:30,761 --> 00:33:33,820
created an ingenious alternative.

528
00:33:33,821 --> 00:33:38,420
He called it the automatic continuous clothing closure.

529
00:33:38,421 --> 00:33:41,200
It closed fabric with a series of small clasps

530
00:33:41,201 --> 00:33:43,560
that joined when a string was pulled.

531
00:33:43,561 --> 00:33:47,540
But design flaws derailed its popularity.

532
00:33:47,541 --> 00:33:51,600
In 1914, Swedish engineer Gideon Sundback

533
00:33:51,601 --> 00:33:53,240
Patten did a more functional version

534
00:33:53,241 --> 00:33:57,200
by more than doubling the number of teeth per inch,

535
00:33:57,201 --> 00:34:01,720
creating what he called the separable fastener.

536
00:34:01,721 --> 00:34:05,980
The zipper as we know it had finally arrived.

537
00:34:05,981 --> 00:34:09,440
A zipper is composed of two opposing rows of teeth,

538
00:34:09,441 --> 00:34:11,160
each with a hook and an indentation.

539
00:34:13,920 --> 00:34:16,220
These tracks mesh when the latching mechanism known

540
00:34:16,221 --> 00:34:19,240
as the slide pulls the two strips of teeth together.

541
00:34:21,600 --> 00:34:28,240
This forces each hook into the offset indentation on the opposite track, securely interlocking the teeth.

542
00:34:29,920 --> 00:34:35,040
Our number two gadget was first applied to items like tobacco pouches and overshoes.

543
00:34:36,120 --> 00:34:40,082
It finally got its familiar name in the 1920s, when the B.F.

544
00:34:40,083 --> 00:34:45,100
Goodrich Company created the term zipper to promote a new brand of galoshes.

545
00:34:46,400 --> 00:34:50,540
Those zippered boots also gave the 20s one of its signature slang terms.

546
00:34:50,541 --> 00:34:57,540
The flapper has become a term associated with young girls in the 1920s.

547
00:34:57,541 --> 00:34:59,540
And these were modern girls.

548
00:34:59,541 --> 00:35:03,540
They were starting to really enter the workplace and take public transportation.

549
00:35:03,541 --> 00:35:05,540
And they were wearing these overshoes.

550
00:35:05,541 --> 00:35:09,540
And they did not always zip up their galoshes completely.

551
00:35:09,541 --> 00:35:11,540
And therefore they would flap around.

552
00:35:11,541 --> 00:35:14,540
And they became known as flappers.

553
00:35:14,541 --> 00:35:22,880
In the 1930s, the original patent for the zipper expired, unzipping the floodgates for

554
00:35:22,881 --> 00:35:24,367
the garment industry.

555
00:35:24,368 --> 00:35:29,180
Zippers evolved from simple clothing closures to fashion statements.

556
00:35:29,181 --> 00:35:35,820
And they're now found on everything from wetsuits to handbags. But manufacturing a

557
00:35:35,821 --> 00:35:40,540
gadget so small and precise requires machinery with little tolerance for error.

558
00:35:40,541 --> 00:35:43,040
Position is very critical.

559
00:35:43,041 --> 00:35:46,540
Some of the die sets are down to almost a hundredths of an inch.

560
00:35:46,541 --> 00:35:50,780
If we're off a little bit, it can cause a zipper to open up or it can cause a zipper

561
00:35:50,781 --> 00:35:53,280
to be too tight and not functional.

562
00:35:53,281 --> 00:35:58,380
But the zippers emerging from the world's factories are generally finely tuned models

563
00:35:58,381 --> 00:36:00,920
of efficiency.

564
00:36:00,921 --> 00:36:05,140
And for all the zipping and unzipping we do, you can see why this humble gadget earned

565
00:36:05,141 --> 00:36:10,060
its number two spot on our countdown.

566
00:36:10,061 --> 00:36:13,300
Ready to zip up this countdown?

567
00:36:13,301 --> 00:36:17,600
The only gadget yet to be unveiled is our number one pick.

568
00:36:17,601 --> 00:36:20,620
These are some of its most primitive ancestors.

569
00:36:20,621 --> 00:36:21,720
Any guesses what it is?

570
00:36:26,340 --> 00:36:30,820
We've been digging through our treasure chest of gizmos and doodads,

571
00:36:30,821 --> 00:36:33,660
counting down our top 10 favorites,

572
00:36:33,661 --> 00:36:36,440
and we're finally ready to uncover the ultimate gadget

573
00:36:36,441 --> 00:36:38,400
that tops them all.

574
00:36:38,401 --> 00:36:41,280
But first, let's take inventory of the finalists

575
00:36:41,281 --> 00:36:43,220
that made our cut.

576
00:36:43,221 --> 00:36:45,720
Number 10, the Rube Goldberg machine.

577
00:36:45,721 --> 00:36:47,660
Action.

578
00:36:47,661 --> 00:36:51,220
Number 9, the corkscrew.

579
00:36:51,221 --> 00:36:53,420
Number 8, the Tealink sensor.

580
00:36:53,421 --> 00:36:55,960
You can detect even the breathing of a person

581
00:36:55,961 --> 00:36:57,700
who has been hidden behind a wall.

582
00:36:57,701 --> 00:36:59,420
Number 7, Furby.

583
00:37:03,260 --> 00:37:06,600
Number 6, Ben Franklin's glass harmonica.

584
00:37:06,601 --> 00:37:09,240
I'd really like to get this instrument off of the endangered

585
00:37:09,241 --> 00:37:12,140
musical instrument list.

586
00:37:12,141 --> 00:37:15,780
Number five, Brent Farley's unsold gadgets.

587
00:37:15,781 --> 00:37:17,220
But I reek with imagination.

588
00:37:17,221 --> 00:37:19,120
I just reek with it.

589
00:37:19,121 --> 00:37:22,020
Number four, the Etch-a-Sketch.

590
00:37:22,021 --> 00:37:25,960
Etch-a-Sketch, the toy you'll never tire of.

591
00:37:25,961 --> 00:37:28,780
Number three, the Zippo lighter.

592
00:37:28,781 --> 00:37:30,660
Works every time.

593
00:37:30,661 --> 00:37:34,500
Number two, the zipper.

594
00:37:34,501 --> 00:37:38,100
And now, the gadget that inspired Ralph Waldo Emerson

595
00:37:38,101 --> 00:37:40,740
to muse that if you build a better one,

596
00:37:40,741 --> 00:37:43,860
the world will beat a path to your door.

597
00:37:43,861 --> 00:37:48,400
The ever-evolving device that helps define man's ingenuity,

598
00:37:48,401 --> 00:37:49,101
the mousetrap.

599
00:37:51,820 --> 00:37:54,020
Over the centuries, our crowning gadget

600
00:37:54,021 --> 00:37:57,800
has come in countless forms and has killed millions of mice

601
00:37:57,801 --> 00:38:00,140
every year.

602
00:38:00,141 --> 00:38:01,860
But it's not the mousetrap's body count

603
00:38:01,861 --> 00:38:03,520
that makes it our number one pick.

604
00:38:04,740 --> 00:38:07,160
It caps off our countdown because it epitomizes

605
00:38:07,161 --> 00:38:09,180
better than any other gadget,

606
00:38:09,181 --> 00:38:11,740
our inborn urge to solve problems

607
00:38:11,741 --> 00:38:13,780
using technology and innovation.

608
00:38:16,380 --> 00:38:19,020
The version we know best is called the Snap Trap.

609
00:38:20,200 --> 00:38:22,340
A Snap Trap's anatomy typically includes

610
00:38:22,341 --> 00:38:24,520
a pine board for the base,

611
00:38:24,521 --> 00:38:26,820
a metal killer bar or striker,

612
00:38:26,821 --> 00:38:29,180
driven by a 15 gauge coil spring,

613
00:38:29,181 --> 00:38:33,580
a thin metal trigger rod, and a bait pedal.

614
00:38:34,320 --> 00:38:36,240
When the kill bar is pulled back,

615
00:38:36,920 --> 00:38:38,220
tension is applied to the spring.

616
00:38:39,340 --> 00:38:41,800
The trigger rod holds the kill bar in place

617
00:38:41,801 --> 00:38:45,000
until a mouse steps on the bait pedal and releases the latch.

618
00:38:47,900 --> 00:38:50,380
More advanced versions of our number one gadget

619
00:38:50,381 --> 00:38:53,580
include Kness Manufacturing's Catch-All,

620
00:38:54,080 --> 00:38:56,780
which adds a new twist by capturing mice alive.

621
00:38:56,781 --> 00:39:04,500
When you take a trap and you wind it up, what you're doing is putting torque on that spring.

622
00:39:08,020 --> 00:39:12,500
The catch-all uses no bait, relying instead on expert placement.

623
00:39:14,820 --> 00:39:20,420
To protect itself, a mouse instinctively moves along walls, looking for nooks to hide in.

624
00:39:23,020 --> 00:39:26,120
When it enters the catch-all, it steps on the trigger.

625
00:39:26,780 --> 00:39:30,020
The trigger activates the spring-loaded three-sided paddle

626
00:39:30,021 --> 00:39:32,960
to rotate around the crossbar.

627
00:39:32,961 --> 00:39:35,100
The paddle scoops up the mouse and flips it

628
00:39:35,101 --> 00:39:37,840
into the holding compartment.

629
00:39:37,841 --> 00:39:41,600
Then the trap's ready to catch another mouse.

630
00:39:41,601 --> 00:39:46,400
Another mouse, another mouse, another mouse, and another mouse,

631
00:39:46,401 --> 00:39:51,600
up to 15 to 20 miles, dependable on how it's wound up.

632
00:39:51,601 --> 00:39:54,960
Another non-baited Kness mouse trap, the Pro Catch,

633
00:39:54,961 --> 00:39:57,480
uses a double-entry teeter-totter trigger

634
00:39:57,481 --> 00:40:01,320
to let the mice in and keep them in.

635
00:40:01,321 --> 00:40:03,120
When a mouse enters the ProCatch,

636
00:40:03,121 --> 00:40:07,500
it scurries onto a platform hinged at the center.

637
00:40:07,501 --> 00:40:09,600
As the mouse passes the pivot point,

638
00:40:09,601 --> 00:40:13,380
its weight causes the platform to tilt on its hinge,

639
00:40:13,381 --> 00:40:18,380
which reveals an opening into the trap's holding compartment.

640
00:40:18,381 --> 00:40:21,040
When the mouse steps off the platform,

641
00:40:21,041 --> 00:40:24,020
the platform falls back into its set position,

642
00:40:24,021 --> 00:40:26,520
and the exit is blocked.

643
00:40:26,521 --> 00:40:32,260
It is a live, humane trap, but you have to service the trap.

644
00:40:32,261 --> 00:40:34,320
If you want to keep him alive, you

645
00:40:34,321 --> 00:40:37,340
put a little water or a little apple,

646
00:40:37,341 --> 00:40:40,200
giving something for nourishment.

647
00:40:40,201 --> 00:40:43,200
This 18th century mouse trap is much less concerned

648
00:40:43,201 --> 00:40:46,420
with a mouse's welfare.

649
00:40:46,421 --> 00:40:49,020
This is a deadfall trap.

650
00:40:49,021 --> 00:40:52,300
It has three blocks of wood, which

651
00:40:52,301 --> 00:41:00,440
held up by a burlap string and when the mouse or rat chew through the burlap string, the

652
00:41:00,441 --> 00:41:03,600
block of wood will come down and crush them.

653
00:41:03,601 --> 00:41:11,800
Vintage or modern, the Mouse Trap exemplifies everything we love about gadgets.

654
00:41:11,801 --> 00:41:16,580
It not only gets the job done, but does it in a way that reminds us just how clever we

655
00:41:16,581 --> 00:41:18,580
can be.

656
00:41:18,581 --> 00:41:20,580
and all the finalists on our countdown

657
00:41:20,581 --> 00:41:24,780
are proof positive of the boundlessness of human ingenuity.

